u", "lp": -0.003896725829690695, "top": [["-gnu", -0.003896725829690695], ["-gun", -5.724969863891602]]}, {"t": "`)", "lp": -0.028194433078169823, "top": [["`)", -0.028194433078169823], ["`", -4.199943542480469]]}, {"t": "\n\n", "lp": -0.0023677940480411053, "top": [["\n\n", -0.0023677940480411053], [" \u2014", -6.183635711669922]]}, {"t": "```", "lp": -0.023458685725927353, "top": [["```", -0.023458685725927353], ["##", -4.229495048522949]]}, {"t": "bash", "lp": -0.0005940771079622209, "top": [["bash", -0.0005940771079622209], ["\n", -8.219571113586426]]}, {"t": "\n", "lp": -0.0014332069549709558, "top": [["\n", -0.0014332069549709558], [" -", -8.528268814086914]]}, {"t": "-", "lp": -0.0007526129484176636, "top": [["-", -0.0007526129484176636], [" -", -7.8300557136535645]]}], "text": "# PKGBUILD build() Function: Step-by-Step Explanation\n\nLet me break down this `build()` function line by line.\n\n## The Setup\n\n```bash\nsource /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh --force\n```\nThis loads the Intel oneAPI environment variables (compiler paths, library paths, etc.). The `--force` flag forces the environment to be set up even if it appears already configured. This is essential because we're compiling with Intel's `icx`/`icpx` compilers, not the default GCC.\n\n```bash\nset -e\n```\nThis makes the script exit immediately if any command returns a non-zero (error) status. A safety net so a failed command doesn't cascade into broken output.\n\n## The CMake Configuration\n\nNow the main `cmake` command. Let me flag by flag:\n\n```bash\ncmake -S \"${srcdir}/llama.cpp\" -B \"${srcdir}/build\" -G Ninja\n```\n- `-S \"${srcdir}/llama.cpp\"` \u2014 **source directory** (where the source code lives)\n- `-B \"${srcdir}/build\"` \u2014 **build directory** (where compiled artifacts go)\n- `-G Ninja` \u2014 use the **Ninja** build system (fast, minimal output) instead of Make\n\n```bash\n-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release\n```\nCompile with **optimizations enabled** (`-O3`-level flags) and no debug symbols. This is the production build.\n\n```bash\n-DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr -DCMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR=lib\n```\n- Install binaries to `/usr`\n- Install libraries to `/usr/lib` (rather than the default `/usr/lib64` or `/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu`)\n\n```bash\n-"}